1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,488
One tester left a gaming PC alone for six months, then cleaned out the dust.

2
00:00:05,768 --> 00:00:13,778
The processor ran 11 degrees cooler in games, and the graphics card about 4 degrees cooler, with nothing else changed.

3
00:00:14,058 --> 00:00:22,905
In the next few minutes we'll clean one dusty PC together, in the right order, and I'll show you the three ways people break hardware while doing it.

4
00:00:24,655 --> 00:00:26,426
Here's why dust matters at all.

5
00:00:26,706 --> 00:00:32,123
Every fan in your case pulls air across thin metal fins, and the heat leaves with that air.

6
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:36,440
Dust settles on the fins and in the filters like a felt blanket.

7
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,330
Less air gets through, so the chips get hotter.

8
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:44,004
Modern processors and graphics cards don't just get warm and carry on.

9
00:00:44,284 --> 00:00:50,155
They protect themselves by lowering their boost clocks, which means fewer frames and louder fans.

10
00:00:50,435 --> 00:00:55,173
So a dusty PC is often a slower PC, not just a dirtier one.

11
00:00:55,923 --> 00:01:00,480
Our PC is a normal tower that's been running for a year in a bedroom.

12
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:03,373
Before we touch it, shut it down from Windows.

13
00:01:03,653 --> 00:01:11,531
Then flip the switch on the back of the power supply to the off position, the side marked with a circle, and pull the power cord out.

14
00:01:11,811 --> 00:01:16,263
With the cord out, press and hold the power button for about 30 seconds.

15
00:01:16,543 --> 00:01:19,900
That drains the little bit of charge still sitting in the system.

16
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:24,081
Now carry the PC somewhere with a hard floor and some airflow.

17
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:31,813
A garage, a balcony or a table by an open window is perfect, because the dust is about to go everywhere.

18
00:01:32,563 --> 00:01:35,202
The first way people break a PC is static.

19
00:01:35,482 --> 00:01:41,432
Your body can carry a charge you never feel, and a spark you can't even see can damage a chip.

20
00:01:41,712 --> 00:01:48,109
So stand on a hard floor, not carpet, and touch the bare metal of the case before you reach inside.

21
00:01:48,389 --> 00:01:50,692
Touch it again now and then while you work.

22
00:01:50,972 --> 00:01:55,463
An antistatic wrist strap clipped to the case is even better, and it's cheap.

23
00:01:55,743 --> 00:02:00,801
This is also why a household vacuum cleaner should never go inside the case.

24
00:02:01,081 --> 00:02:07,977
Its plastic nozzle builds up static as air rushes through it, and it's easy to knock a small part off the board.

25
00:02:08,257 --> 00:02:16,632
Quick favor before we start cleaning: over 99 percent of the watch time on this channel comes from people who aren't subscribed.

26
00:02:16,912 --> 00:02:22,426
If this saves your PC, subscribing is free, and it tells me to keep making these.

27
00:02:23,176 --> 00:02:26,171
Start with the easy wins: the dust filters.

28
00:02:26,451 --> 00:02:32,741
Most cases have a mesh filter at the front, one under the power supply, and often one on top.

29
00:02:33,021 --> 00:02:35,846
They usually slide out or pull off with magnets.

30
00:02:36,126 --> 00:02:40,170
Here the vacuum is fine, because the filter is out of the PC.

31
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:43,689
Most mesh filters can also be rinsed under the tap.

32
00:02:43,969 --> 00:02:48,040
If you wash one, let it dry completely before it goes back in.

33
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:53,436
Water and electronics only mix when you give them a chance, so don't rush this part.

34
00:02:54,186 --> 00:02:58,005
Now the compressed air, and the second way people break things.

35
00:02:58,285 --> 00:03:04,466
Point the air at a fan and it spins like a pinwheel, far faster than it was ever built to run.

36
00:03:04,746 --> 00:03:07,888
That can wear out its bearings or even crack a blade.

37
00:03:08,168 --> 00:03:13,420
So hold every fan still with a finger or a plastic tool before you blow on it.

38
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:20,118
Use short bursts, not one long blast, and keep the nozzle about 5 to 10 centimetres away.

39
00:03:20,398 --> 00:03:21,938
Keep the can upright, too.

40
00:03:22,218 --> 00:03:28,239
Tip it sideways or upside down and it can spit freezing liquid propellant onto your parts.

41
00:03:28,989 --> 00:03:30,103
The order matters.

42
00:03:30,383 --> 00:03:38,056
Work from the top of the case down, so loose dust falls toward the bottom and out, not onto parts you've already cleaned.

43
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:44,494
On the processor cooler and the graphics card, blow from the side where the air normally leaves the heatsink.

44
00:03:44,774 --> 00:03:50,140
That pushes dust back out the way it came in, instead of packing it deeper into the fins.

45
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:55,695
Clean the case fans, then the cooler, then the graphics card, then the floor of the case.

46
00:03:55,975 --> 00:03:59,696
Ten minutes of this and you'll see the difference with your own eyes.

47
00:04:00,446 --> 00:04:03,503
The third way people break things is going too deep.

48
00:04:03,783 --> 00:04:05,679
Never open the power supply.

49
00:04:05,959 --> 00:04:12,956
Its capacitors can hold a dangerous charge even after it's unplugged, and there's nothing inside you need to clean.

50
00:04:13,236 --> 00:04:15,741
Just blow through its vents from the outside.

51
00:04:16,021 --> 00:04:18,375
Don't take the graphics card apart either.

52
00:04:18,655 --> 00:04:27,462
And don't take the processor cooler off just to dust it, because if you lift it, the thermal paste underneath has to be cleaned off and replaced.

53
00:04:27,742 --> 00:04:30,569
On the motherboard and the memory, use air only.

54
00:04:30,849 --> 00:04:33,022
Brushes can snag tiny parts there.

55
00:04:33,772 --> 00:04:38,413
Some dust turns into sticky grime, especially near smokers or kitchens.

56
00:04:38,693 --> 00:04:46,479
For that, put a little isopropyl alcohol on a cotton swab or a microfibre cloth, wipe, and let it evaporate.

57
00:04:46,759 --> 00:04:49,445
Never spray anything directly into the case.

58
00:04:49,725 --> 00:04:56,518
Skip household cleaners with bleach or ammonia, and skip paper towels, which shed fibres and scratch.

59
00:04:56,798 --> 00:04:58,529
And wear a simple dust mask.

60
00:04:58,809 --> 00:05:02,083
A year of dust is not something you want in your lungs.

61
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:07,322
Before you put the side panel back on, plug the PC in and turn it on.

62
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:08,903
Look at every fan.

63
00:05:09,183 --> 00:05:12,345
Each one should spin, and none should rattle or wobble.

64
00:05:12,625 --> 00:05:16,679
A fan that stays still might have a loose cable you knocked while cleaning.

65
00:05:16,959 --> 00:05:20,449
Then close it up and play the same game you played before.

66
00:05:20,729 --> 00:05:24,355
Watch the temperatures with a free monitoring tool and compare.

67
00:05:24,635 --> 00:05:28,097
For that tester, this is where the 11 degrees showed up.

68
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:36,431
To keep it clean for longer, check the filters every month or two, and do a full clean like this every three to six months.

69
00:05:36,711 --> 00:05:41,105
With pets, smokers or a dusty room, make it every two to three months.

70
00:05:41,385 --> 00:05:43,743
Keep the tower off the carpet if you can.

71
00:05:44,023 --> 00:05:50,171
And set your fans for slight positive pressure, which means more fans pulling air in than pushing it out.

72
00:05:50,451 --> 00:05:58,093
A common setup is two or three intake fans at the front, each behind a filter, and one exhaust fan at the back.

73
00:05:58,373 --> 00:06:03,246
Then most air enters through the filters, and less dust sneaks in through every gap.

74
00:06:03,996 --> 00:06:06,112
Now your PC is clean and cool.

75
00:06:06,392 --> 00:06:14,509
And if you're wondering which graphics card deserves that clean case, this Saturday we rank every budget card you can buy right now.

76
00:06:14,789 --> 00:06:22,382
If this helped, share it with a friend whose PC sounds like a jet engine, subscribe, and comment your questions below.

